LOCATION:Virtual\, and Clackamas Education Service District
SUMMARY:Healthy Cross Cultural Teams - Pre K-12 (Hybrid)
DESCRIPTION:Educators need systematic ways to engage each other on how cu
 lture and race shape all aspects of their work—from curriculum and inst
 ruction to assessment\, feedback\, family and community relationships\, a
 nd cross-cultural collaboration with colleagues.  This week-long\, credit
 -optional workshop for school-based Pre K-12 educators will help individu
 als and teams examine their positionality and racial socialization throug
 h critical reflection.  Participants will explore how racial identity dev
 elopment and cultural literacy strengthen relationships with colleagues\,
  students\, and communities\, and together build skills for disrupting in
 equitable systems through cross-cultural solidarity.Participants in this 
 course will strengthen their skills around:      Understanding how our ra
 cial identity impacts our work in schools and the role of racial literacy
  in creating healthy identity development          Developing processes t
 o nurture and grow principled relationships across race          Sustaini
 ng cross-racial solidarity while navigating institutional realities and r
 esistance    Action planning to create a vision and values to help guide 
 the work of cross-racial teams\, and develop next steps to continue the w
 ork back at their sites  Course Details &amp\; RegistrationDates and Time
 s: Monday-Friday\, June 22-26\, 2026In-Person: June 22\, 24\, 26\, 2026\,
  9 a.m.- 4 p.m.\, Clackamas Education Service District\, 13455 SE 97th Av
 enue\, Clackamas\, OR&#160\;Virtual:&#160\;June 23\, 25\, 2026\, 9:30-11:
 30 a.m.Instructors: Elizabeth Denevi\, PhD\, and Jasmine LockeGraduate Co
 ntinuing Education credit: 2 semester hours\, $770Registration Informatio
 n Coming SoonAbout the InstructorsElizabeth Denevi\, PhD\, is an assistan
 t clinical professor at Lewis &amp\; Clark Graduate School of Education a
 nd Counseling. She has served as a classroom teacher as well as an equity
  director\, leadership coach\, and director of professional development. 
 She is co-author Learning and Teaching While White: Antiracist Strategies
  for School Communities (with Jenna Chandler Ward)&#160\;as well as Integ
 rating Educator Well-Being\, Growth\, and Evaluation: Four Foundations fo
 r Leaders (with Lori Cohen)\, both featured titles in Routledge's Equity 
 and Social Justice in Education series.Jasmine Locke is an equity-centere
 d educational leader and doctoral candidate at Lewis &amp\; Clark Graduat
 e School of Education and Counseling. Her research explores racial litera
 cy\, socialization\, and the educational trajectories of students engaged
  in Black Student Unions. With a decade of experience as a classroom teac
 her\, school support specialist and equity coach\, she brings deep expert
 ise in culturally responsive pedagogy\, student identity development\, an
 d systems-level change. She is featured in Teach for Climate Justice: A V
 ision for Transforming Education\, highlighting her commitment to joy as 
 a form of resistance.New workshops and trainings are added to our calenda
 r regularly. For the latest on professional development related to your s
 pecific interests\, sign up for our mailing list! (https://lclark.tfaform
 s.net/4735441)
